# =============================================================================
# Odal Node — .gitignore
# Public repo: github.com/odal-node/dpp-core
# =============================================================================

# Keycloak dev realm (generated from template)
ops/docker/keycloak/*-realm.json
!ops/docker/keycloak/*-realm.template.json

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRETS & ENVIRONMENT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets/
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUST
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/target/
# The product-group plugins are excluded from the main workspace (different toolchain
# target) and form their own workspace at plugins/Cargo.toml, with its own
# shared target dir. /target/ above only anchors to the repo root, so it's
# listed explicitly; the second pattern catches any pre-restructuring
# per-member target dirs left over locally.
plugins/target/
plugins/*/target/
**/*.rs.bak

# `Cargo.lock` is deliberately NOT ignored. The old "libraries don't commit a
# lockfile" advice predates the current guidance and cost this repo a property
# it needs more than a library usually does: without a committed lockfile,
# `--locked` means nothing and `cargo audit` scans a graph resolved fresh on
# every run, so an advisory can appear or vanish between two CI runs over an
# identical tree. A regulatory library whose dependency set is not reproducible
# cannot say what it was built from.
#
# The cost is real and accepted: a lockfile conflicts on concurrent dependency
# changes, and this repo's strict-up-to-date rule already serialises merges.
# Resolve a conflict by regenerating (`cargo update -w`), never by hand.
#
# Testing against the newest dependencies moves to the scheduled advisory run
# rather than happening implicitly on every build.
#
# Three lockfiles are tracked, one per workspace: the root, `plugins/` (the ten
# product-group crates share a workspace) and `fuzz/`.
#
# The per-plugin `plugins/*/Cargo.lock` files are **not** among them. They are
# leftovers from before those crates shared a workspace and no longer govern
# anything — `plugins/Cargo.lock` does. They are left on disk rather than
# deleted here, since removing them is not this change's business, but
# committing them would enshrine ten dead files as though they were policy.
plugins/*/Cargo.lock

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EDITOR & OS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BUILD ARTIFACTS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.log
*.tmp
*.bak
coverage/
.nyc_output/
# Keystores — `KeyStore::open` creates the file if absent, so never let one
# land in the tree.
keys.json
*.keystore.json

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CI ORACLES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The GS1 syntax oracle installs its engine here. CI-only: no published crate
# depends on it, and `cargo build` never sees it.
.github/scripts/node_modules/
.github/scripts/package-lock.json
